That's a common hiccup — usually it means GitHub hasn't detected the workflow file yet, most likely because the file ended up in the wrong folder path. Here's how to check and fix it:

Troubleshoot
Go to your repo's main page (click the repo name at the top)
Look at the file/folder list. You should see a .github folder. Click into it → you should see workflows → inside that, wind_alert.yml
✅ If you see .github/workflows/wind_alert.yml
The path is correct. Try:

Hard-refresh the Actions tab (Ctrl+Shift+R on Windows, Cmd+Shift+R on Mac)
Wait 30 seconds and refresh again — GitHub can take a moment to pick up new workflows
❌ If the file is somewhere else (e.g. sitting in the root folder, or the folder name is slightly wrong)
This is the most likely issue. When you typed .github/workflows/wind_alert.yml in the filename box, GitHub should have auto-created the nested folders. If it didn't, the file might just be named literally .github/workflows/wind_alert.yml in the root. Here's how to fix it:

Delete the misplaced file — click on it, then click the trash can icon (top right of the file view), then commit the deletion

Re-create it properly:

From the repo main page, click "Add file" → "Create new file"
In the filename box, type: .github then press / — you'll see it turn into a folder
Then type: workflows then press / again — another folder
Then type: wind_alert.yml
The path shown above the box should read: .github/workflows/wind_alert.yml
Paste the YAML content from Step 5, File 3
Click "Commit changes"
Now go back to the Actions tab — you should see "Jericho Beach Wind Alert" in the left sidebar

The key trick is pressing / after each folder name so GitHub recognizes them as folders rather than part of the filename. Let me know if it shows up!








